## Purpose

Remove global discovery of private relay recipients and operational metadata while preserving deliberate public discovery surfaces.

## Work

- Change production peer listing so authentication alone does not enumerate private aliases, session IDs, host IDs, keys, versions, OS data, lease timing, or reservation metadata.
- Define owner/admin visibility and capability-authorised resolution exactly as approved in the protocol design.
- Keep public/gated/unlisted room discovery semantics explicit and prevent room endpoints from becoming a side channel for private recipient metadata.
- Make enumeration-resistant errors and timing behaviour practical; avoid distinguishable “unknown recipient” versus “known but unauthorised” responses where the design requires privacy.
- Preserve explicit development observability only behind unmistakable dev configuration.

## Acceptance evidence

- Authenticated unauthorised peers cannot list, resolve, or infer a private recipient through list, send errors, room APIs, lease metadata, or related endpoints.
- Owners/admins and deliberately public surfaces retain only the intended visibility.
- In-memory/SQLite and HTTP route-auth matrix tests cover the new policy.

## Post-B219 seams (from B261)

- Ordinary peer discovery: `R.list_peers` / `handle_list` / `RegistrationLease.to_json` — needs explicit private/owner/admin views at the backend, not only response redaction.
- Existence oracle: `handle_pubkey` via `identity_pk_of` / enc / signed_at / sig.
- Do not leak private destinations through room rosters/errors; keep deliberate public/gated/unlisted/private room semantics otherwise unchanged.
- Design input: `.collab/research/2026-07-22-b261-private-reachability-persistence-reconciliation.md`.

## Incremental discovery/oracle TDD seam requirements

B264 must add a dedicated failing suite (proposed `ocaml/test/test_relay_private_discovery.ml`) before implementation. Cover both backends and HTTP where applicable:

- ordinary authenticated `/list` excludes private leases while owner/admin views remain explicit;
- `/pubkey/<private-alias>` does not reveal existence/keys to unrelated peers;
- registration/lease JSON, errors, health/stats, diagnostics, room directories/rosters/history/knocks cannot reconstruct a usable private DM route;
- deliberate public/gated/unlisted/private room visibility remains unchanged except that roster aliases do not become DM authority;
- unknown/private/unauthorised resolution responses are practically uniform;
- alias visibility policy is stored at the backend/domain seam, not response-only redaction.

Reuse existing auth/list/room/landing contract suites; do not treat the accidental unreviewed `.worktrees/b264-private-discovery` changes as accepted implementation. Preserved recovery seed exists only for coordinator evaluation.

## Independent review findings requiring disposition

- Audit every key lookup, not only `/pubkey`: `enc_pubkey_of`, identity/signature metadata helpers, and any handler wrapper must respect private visibility or remain owner/admin-only. Add oracle tests for each exposed endpoint/caller.
- InMemory visibility is volatile; production security claims must be tied to migration-enabled durable state/SQLite construction, with tokenless/test backends explicitly excluded from production guarantees.